[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) is marked known: one bug, one fix queued. Its reports say otherwise.
Read by tenant, channel and body, they describe three problems with three different fixes. The
queued parser fix closes only one of them.

<Callout kind="warn">
Do not close #4471's reports when the parser fix ships. The <code>hint</code> and
<code>one-day</code> reports below will keep coming.
</Callout>

## The split

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} issues={{
    parser: 'https://github.com/jbandi/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/1',
    hint: 'https://github.com/jbandi/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/2',
    'one-day': 'https://github.com/jbandi/mega-s01e03-support-kb/issues/3',
  }} />

## How each report was placed

1. **One report per id.** The export repeats some reports across its pages; each id counts once.
2. **`one-day`** — the body says the booking moved by one day, not that day and month swapped:
   "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May" (R-41202), "Client in Lisbon got
   Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday" (R-41344), "The invite arrived before I made the
   booking" (R-41236). A day/month flip cannot do that. The desk already lists these in
   `desk-notes.json`; the #4471 page files them under `parser` as a footnote.
3. **`hint`** — a day-first tenant on `booking-v3`, reporting through the form. That form prints
   `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field, people type month first as told, and the form reads the
   tenant's way. The parser fix does not touch it.
4. **`parser`** — every other report from a day-first tenant: app, email, and forms that print
   `DD/MM/YYYY`. This is the bug #4471 is named for.

The intake tag (`meta.intakeTag`) is not used. It is guessed from the reporter's browser, and it
disagrees with the tenant's locale and form often (see the tag column on [#4471](/kb/incidents/4471)).

## Why they were merged

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line alone. All three
problems produce the same subjects ("Wrong day on the invite", "Date is wrong"), so all three
landed in #4471 and inherited its "known" status.

